.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO_POOLED 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do_pooled \- perform an HTTP request using the shared Tier 2 engine, blocking until it completes
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_do_pooled(chttpcli cli,
                                    const chttp_request_t *req,
                                    chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A thin wrapper over chttpclient_do_async: submits the request to the
shared engine, blocks until it completes, and returns the exact same
ccol_retval_t / resp_out call shape chttpclient_do uses; but the
connect/write/read work happens on the engine's own reactor threads
rather than the calling thread, and concurrent callers across many
chttpcli handles share one small, fixed-size reactor thread pool
instead of each blocking its own OS thread for the duration of its
request.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli      Client handle; must not be NULL.
req      Request to execute; must not be NULL.
resp_out Receives the response on success; must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Same result codes as chttpclient_do, with one difference: a failure
to even submit the request to the engine (OOM, or the engine
failing to start) is reported as ccol_unexpected_failure rather than
a more specific code. Everything detected once the request is
actually in flight (bad URL, TLS failure, connection failure,
transfer errors, timeouts, too many redirects) is reported with
the exact same specific codes chttpclient_do uses.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_do (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_async (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming (3)
